  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday) \nIssued at 1035 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThe FA will find itself beneath the influence of an upper level \nridge through at least the start of the extended forecast period. \nWith the ridge axis centered over the ArkLaTx region Monday morning, \namplifying and shifting westward back over the West Texas region by \nmid-week. As a result, will see southwest flow aloft shift westerly \nwith increased geopotential heights. Thickness values will also \nincrease, thanks to a an H7 area of high pressure along with a lee \nsurface low over southeastern CO. In response, warm southwesterly \nwinds will work to transport WAA into the region working in \ncombination with the increased height values and mostly clear skies \nto bring temperatures back to near normal Monday and Tuesday, then \nabove normal Wednesday with highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s. The \nforecast remains dry through the work week, though NWP guidance still \nhints at a weak shortwave translating through the Desert Southwest \nto impact portions of the Panhandle region. If this comes to \nfruition, there is the potential areas near the Texas/New Mexico \nstate line might see a few isolated storms and/or showers as they \nmove in from the higher terrain. Though confidence remains low in \nthis at this time and so we will remain a dry forecast for the time \nbeing. However, this may need to be changed in future forecasts. Our \nnext chance for beneficial rainfall looks to be this next weekend, \nwith both ensembles and deterministic guidance in agreement with the \nbase of a trough translating across the Canadian Provinces to swing \na cold front and bring precipitation back to the region. However, \nconsidering this remains several days out, the evolution of this \nsystem is uncertain with the ECMWF showing a more progressive and \nwetter solution. While the GFS is much slower and drier. For now \nwill maintain NBM mentionable PoPs area-wide until we see better \nagreement amongst guidance in the coming days.",
